# THE SHARED-&T UB LEDGER — the standing facts that rule
# `type.shared-ref.write-through-derived-ub` rests on.
#
# The rule: given `r: &T` with T Freeze, the referent is immutable for r's
# lifetime and a write through a pointer DERIVED FROM `r` is undefined. The
# exception that makes it livable: a pointer VALUE loaded OUT of the referent
# carries its own provenance, is not derived from `r`, and may be written
# through — which is exactly what Rc::clone, Arc::clone, Weak::upgrade and
# Rc/Arc::downgrade do.
#
# `shared_ref_ub_lint.sh` holds this file in BOTH directions: an entry that
# appears, disappears, or changes count is RED either way. The number can only
# move by somebody editing this file on purpose, which is the whole point — a
# census that drifts on its own is a measurement nobody is reading.
#
# ⚠ WHY A CENSUS AND NOT AN ASSERTION. Neither section below can be decided by
# reading text. Whether a given laundering site is sound depends on the receiver,
# the pointee's Freeze-ness, and whether the write lands in the referent's own
# bytes or behind a loaded pointer — none of which is visible in the shape. So
# the lint does not judge; it makes the population impossible to change quietly,
# and leaves the judging to the reviewer who adds the line.
#
# ── SECTION `ATOMIC` ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every `pub struct Atomic*` in stdlib/lang/atomic/atomic.logos, and whether its
# declaration contains an `UnsafeCell`.
#
# THIS IS THE CLAUSE THAT WENT SILENTLY FALSE LAST TIME. The comment in
# mlir_gen_fn.cpp asserted for months that atomics "are NOT UnsafeCell-wrapped
# in our stdlib" — it had been false since the commit after it was written, and
# nothing re-measured it. `type_is_freeze` recognises interior mutability ONLY
# by finding an UnsafeCell in the pointee's inline bytes, so a primitive that
# mutates through `&self` without one is classified Freeze, earns
# noalias+readonly, and is miscompiled from -O1 up. A `no` in this column is a
# soundness bug, and the lint reports it on its own terms, not just as a diff.
#
# FORMAT: `<StructName> <yes|no>`
ATOMIC
AtomicBool yes
AtomicI16 yes
AtomicI32 yes
AtomicI64 yes
AtomicI8 yes
AtomicIsize yes
AtomicPtr yes
AtomicU16 yes
AtomicU32 yes
AtomicU64 yes
AtomicU8 yes
AtomicUsize yes
#
# ── SECTION `LAUNDER` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Per-file count of the const->mut laundering shape in stdlib — the shape the
# rule declares undefined to write through.
#
# PATTERN: `as \*const [^;]* as \*mut ` on `//`-comment-stripped lines. It is
# DELIBERATELY BROADER than "same type on both sides": it also catches
# `as *const A as *mut B`, which launders just as well. Over-count, never
# under-count. Comments are stripped because this rule is the most heavily
# documented thing in the tree and a site that EXPLAINS the class must not read
# as an instance of it — otherwise the incentive becomes "document less".
#
# ⚠ WHAT A ROW DOES NOT MEAN. A row is not an accusation. Every site here was
# read and is sound for one of five reasons, none of them textual: a `&mut self`
# receiver (fabric push/set/insert/erase), a by-value `self` (Vec::into_iter,
# PrimVec::drop), a non-Freeze pointee (every writ site — Writ holds UnsafeCell),
# a cast used only to reach a `*mut`-receiver method for a READ (http types /
# serialize / parse, hashmap_at, deem/incr, node_arc_data_ptr), or THE
# LOADED-POINTER EXCEPTION (rc.logos, arc.logos — the refcount lives behind
# `self.inner`, which is loaded, so the write is not derived from the shared
# reference). That audit is a hand read of one syntactic form: it is evidence,
# not a proof of absence, which is precisely why the population is pinned here
# rather than re-derived by hand the next time somebody asks.
#
# ⚠ AND WHAT THIS SECTION CANNOT DO: it says nothing about USER code. Nothing in
# the tree detects the prohibited shape outside stdlib; a real detector must
# model the provenance chain, because a syntactic matcher would fire on every
# Rc/Arc/Weak method above. That is an open question, not an oversight.
#
# FORMAT: `<repo-relative path>\t<count>`  (TAB-separated)
LAUNDER
stdlib/lang/atomic/atomic.logos	2
stdlib/lang/cell/cell.logos	2
stdlib/lang/fabric/fabric.logos	6
stdlib/lang/ptr/ptr.logos	1
stdlib/lang/rc/rc.logos	7
stdlib/lang/writ/array.logos	2
stdlib/lang/writ/decimal.logos	1
stdlib/lang/writ/parameter.logos	1
stdlib/lang/writ/tmpl.logos	1
stdlib/lang/writ/typed_arr.logos	2
stdlib/lang/writ/typed_value.logos	1
stdlib/lang/writ/wmap.logos	4
stdlib/lang/writ/wstring.logos	1
stdlib/lcm/deem/data/bt/node.logos	1
stdlib/mem/collections/hashmap/hashmap.logos	1
stdlib/mem/collections/vec/vec.logos	1
stdlib/mem/deem/incr.logos	5
stdlib/mem/sync/arc.logos	7
stdlib/mem/wql/plan.logos	8
stdlib/mem/writ/clone.logos	1
stdlib/mem/writ/parser.logos	1
stdlib/mem/writ/wbs.logos	2
stdlib/std/io/http/parse.logos	1
stdlib/std/io/http/serialize.logos	1
stdlib/std/io/http/types.logos	5
stdlib/std/os/unix/signal/signal.logos	2
stdlib/std/rt/fiber/thread.logos	1
stdlib/std/time/time.logos	1
